    "result_text": "The full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation by adding a single new file: `src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/OwnerTelephoneLengthAdvice.java`.\n\n**How it works** \u2014 it mirrors the codebase's established `RequestBodyAdvice` pattern (as used by `OwnerUniqueTelephoneAdvice`): on `POST /api/owners`, it normalizes the submitted telephone to E.164 via the existing `OwnerMapper.normalizeTelephone`, then checks the national digit count against its country code (`+61` \u2192 9, `+1` \u2192 10; other codes fall through to the existing generic E.164 pattern). A wrong length throws `WrongTelephoneLengthException`, whose `@ExceptionHandler` returns **400 Bad Request**.\n\n**Key decisions that kept structural impact minimal and correct:**\n- **One new, small, cohesive file** \u2014 no method in any existing class was modified. The advice's methods each have low cyclomatic complexity and near-zero surrounding class complexity, and the file multiplier is 1.\n- I initially tried tightening the `@Pattern` regex on `Owner.telephone`, then **reverted it** \u2014 Hibernate validates that entity constraint at persist time, and a violation there throws `jakarta.validation.ConstraintViolationException`, which the global handler maps to **500, not 400**. The advice guarantees the spec-required 400.\n- Added `@Order(Ordered.HIGHEST_PRECEDENCE)` so the specific exception handler is consulted before the global `Exception`\u2192500 handler (the same technique the sibling telephone advice uses).\n\nThe specified reject case (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and the valid AU-mobile path (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 `+61` + 9 national digits) is unaffected.",
